WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:08.303
A cell with an extra or missing chromosome has the wrong dose of the genes on it.

2
00:00:08.783 --> 00:00:10.163
That can change development.

3
00:00:11.103 --> 00:00:14.583
Most aneuploid human embryos do not survive pregnancy.

4
00:00:15.663 --> 00:00:20.803
Trisomy 21 is one of the few autosomal trisomies seen regularly at birth.

5
00:00:21.623 --> 00:00:26.563
It means three copies of chromosome 21 instead of two, and causes Down syndrome.

6
00:00:27.363 --> 00:00:30.003
The important change is the extra dose of genes.

7
00:00:30.643 --> 00:00:32.723
How does a cell get that extra chromosome?

8
00:00:33.833 --> 00:00:36.633
Extra X chromosomes are better tolerated.

9
00:00:37.633 --> 00:00:43.233
Mammalian cells silence every X beyond one, so extra X chromosomes add little active

10
00:00:43.233 --> 00:00:46.153
dose. But some genes escape this silencing.

11
00:00:47.113 --> 00:00:51.313
Extra active copies of those genes are thought to explain many features of

12
00:00:51.313 --> 00:00:54.533
Klinefelter syndrome, usually an extra X in a male.

13
00:00:55.567 --> 00:00:59.907
Nondisjunction is a failure of chromosomes to part when a cell divides.

14
00:01:00.867 --> 00:01:03.987
At meiosis I, the two homologues of each pair separate.

15
00:01:04.827 --> 00:01:07.427
At meiosis II, the sister chromatids separate.

16
00:01:08.347 --> 00:01:11.507
Follow chromosome 21 in one human spermatocyte.

17
00:01:12.407 --> 00:01:17.127
Assume one error, otherwise normal divisions, and recovery of all four products.

18
00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:23.810
Suppose both homologues go to the same cell at meiosis I. Meiosis II then runs

19
00:01:23.810 --> 00:01:29.150
normally. That cell gives two sperm, each with two copies of chromosome 21.

20
00:01:30.070 --> 00:01:31.210
These are disomic sperm.

21
00:01:32.050 --> 00:01:34.290
The other cell has no chromosome 21.

22
00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:36.390
Its two sperm have none.

23
00:01:37.290 --> 00:01:38.590
These are nullisomic sperm.

24
00:01:39.583 --> 00:01:44.563
Now meiosis I is normal, but sisters fail to part in one cell at meiosis II.

25
00:01:45.323 --> 00:01:48.083
This gives one disomic sperm and one with none.

26
00:01:48.963 --> 00:01:50.723
The other cell gives two normal sperm.

27
00:01:51.683 --> 00:01:56.063
Joined to a normal egg, a disomic sperm makes a zygote with three copies of

28
00:01:56.063 --> 00:02:00.443
chromosome 21. A sperm with none makes a zygote with only one.

29
00:02:01.417 --> 00:02:02.537
That is the model.

30
00:02:03.437 --> 00:02:08.457
In people, most free trisomy 21 comes from the mother, and most maternal errors

31
00:02:08.457 --> 00:02:12.557
arise at meiosis I. The risk rises with the mother's age.

32
00:02:13.477 --> 00:02:15.877
A missed separation changes chromosome number.

33
00:02:16.797 --> 00:02:21.697
The extra or missing genes change the dose, with consequences for development and

34
00:02:21.697 --> 00:02:22.037
survival.
